 ConcurrentHashMap Internal Working in Java – Simplified

As Java developers, we frequently use ConcurrentHashMap for thread-safe operations. But have you ever wondered what makes it so much faster than Hashtable or synchronized HashMap?

Here's a quick breakdown👇
🔹 The Problem
A normal HashMap is not thread-safe.
Map<Integer, String> map = new HashMap<>();

When multiple threads read or write simultaneously, it can lead to data inconsistency.

🔹 How ConcurrentHashMap Solves It
Instead of locking the entire map, ConcurrentHashMap uses a combination of:
✅ CAS (Compare-And-Swap)
✅ Fine-Grained Bucket Locking
✅ Lock-Free Reads
✅ Treeification for Heavy Collisions
✅ Cooperative Resizing

🔹 Lock-Free Reads
map.get(key);
Most get() operations do not require locking.
Multiple threads can read simultaneously without blocking each other, resulting in excellent read performance.

🔹 CAS for Fast Inserts
If a bucket is empty, ConcurrentHashMap uses a CAS operation:
Expected Value = null
Actual Value = null
→ Insert Success
No lock is required.
This significantly reduces contention under high concurrency.

🔹 Fine-Grained Locking
If a bucket already contains data:
Bucket 1 → Locked
Bucket 2 → Available
Bucket 3 → Available
Only the affected bucket is locked, not the entire map.
This allows multiple threads to update different buckets simultaneously.

🔹 Handling Hash Collisions
Initially:
A → B → C → D → E
When a bucket becomes heavily populated (more than 8 nodes), it is converted into a Red-Black Tree.
Benefits:
Linked List : O(n)
Red-Black Tree: O(log n)
Faster lookups and updates under heavy load.

🔹 Smart Resizing
Unlike traditional collections, resizing is not handled by a single thread.
Multiple threads can participate in moving buckets during expansion, improving performance during growth.

🔹 Why It's So Powerful
ConcurrentHashMap achieves thread safety without sacrificing performance by combining:
✔ Lock-Free Reads
✔ CAS Operations
✔ Bucket-Level Locking
✔ Treeification
✔ Cooperative Resizing

This is why it is one of the most widely used concurrent collections in modern Java applications.

💡 Key Takeaway
ConcurrentHashMap doesn't lock the entire table. It uses CAS for simple updates and locks only specific buckets when necessary, enabling high throughput and excellent scalability in multi-threaded applications.

